The built-in assistant module's question field is a module widget, but the assistant view is attached to that same ui view, so the shell frames the whole thing as the model-generated area with its submit boundary - the user's own input appears inside a frame labelled as model-generated. UI spec §4 says a model view sits in a frame labelled as model-generated and that module and model views cannot imitate the trusted chrome; the reverse - a module widget inside the model frame - is the same confusion seen from the other side. Related to k112, which is the identity-space half of the same root cause: the module's own widgets and the model view's widgets share one ui view. Found by the 2026-11-14 work on k109.

2026-11-14 22:10 2026-11-14 research (canlin-scout, read-only, not executed): the frame is per PANE, not per model area. The shell knows exactly one bit per module - sh->mods[k].model, set once for the built-in assistant (src/app/app.c:267) - and layout_pane() draws the ring and reserves a label strip ('Model-generated view', CANLIN_SHELL_MODEL_LABEL 1.5 em) above the view rect (src/shell/shell.c:375-388, draw_pane :1258-1286). The pane contains the module's whole view, so the user's own question field sits under the model-generated label. There is NO submit-boundary chrome anywhere: grepping src/shell/ and src/ui/ for 'submit' finds nothing; the only submit artefacts are the model's own button (src/assistant/view.c:2396-2403) and the payload builder (:1710-1757). So UI §4's 'with its submit boundary visible' is satisfied at best by a widget inside the model view, not by a boundary that marks where model content ends and the trusted surface begins. The shell cannot know which part of a view is the model's: canlin_ui_rect has no owner field, the layout record carries only an ordinal, and there is no region or sub-rectangle concept in the ui layer. The 'views already get arbitrary rectangles' sentence in the shell spec (about canlin#34) supports a second ui VIEW with its own rect, not a region inside one view - one view is one vertical scroll region (UI §2). Options: (a) leave the frame over the whole pane - the module's own input stays under the model label, and the spec has to say so; (b) a region concept in the ui layer - the largest new concept in the question and it buys the least: a frame enclosing a sub-band of a scroll region, which is not a rect the shell owns; (c) a separate ui view for the model view, framed as its own rectangle - the shell frames exactly the model's rect, the module's widgets live outside the frame, and the model's widgets get their own identity space as a side effect; it is the only option that leaves UI §4's sentence true as written ('a view built from the GUI tool sits in a frame labelled as model-generated'); (d) draw the model's input as trusted chrome - inverts the module-roles model, contradicts the assistant-view capability and LLM/UI §5's requirement that the submission boundary remain visible. The spec sentences that would have to change, whichever option is chosen: UI §4 (:132-134), UI §2 (:30), shell §3 (:53-54), shell §1 (:20-21) and LLM/UI §5 (:161, :166).

2026-11-14 22:29 2026-11-14, decided by the owner together with canlin#112: the model view gets its own canlin_ui_view, and the shell lays out two rectangles in the pane - the model's with the frame and the label strip, the module's own without. The work is staged in three cards, one per lane, in the order that keeps the tree working: canlin#128 (the shell holds a second view and frames it), canlin#129 (the app creates the model's view and hands it to the shell), canlin#130 (the binding draws into the model's view). The measurement that preceded the decision is on canlin#112: the priming is confirmed by execution, and the frame's half is what the separate view fixes. What the spec needs to say when it lands: UI §4 ('a view built from the GUI tool sits in a frame labelled as model-generated') becomes true as written; UI §2 ('View = the module's content area, one vertical scroll region owned by the core') needs a companion sentence for the model's view; shell §1 ('One view per module instance') needs one for the second view; shell §3's model-view frame bullet needs to say what is framed and where the submit boundary is drawn - there is still no submit-boundary chrome anywhere, only the model's own submit button, so that sentence has to say what 'the submit boundary is visible' means concretely.
